102 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 54 0 R
/E (Imagine a customer standing at a supermarket checkout. The customer wants the convenience of paying by card but also wants to ensure that the card does not facilitate transactions prohibited by Islamic Shariah. This simple situation illustrates one of the important challenges facing the modern era: how can financial institutions provide modern payment facilities while remaining compliant with Islamic principles?)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
103 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 54 0 R
/E (As technology develops and electronic payments become increasingly common, Islamic financial institutions have introduced Islamic credit cards and Islamic charge cards as alternatives to conventional payment cards. These products aim to provide customers with convenient payment facilities while avoiding prohibited elements such as Riba \(interest\), excessive Gharar \(uncertainty\), and other impermissible financial practices. However, the use of different contractual structures and the existence of different scholarly opinions have created continuing questions regarding their Shariah compliance.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
104 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 54 0 R
/E (This research examines the concepts, development, operational structures, Shariah principles, and contemporary practices of Islamic credit cards and Islamic charge cards. It compares these products with conventional credit and charge cards and examines commonly used Islamic contracts, including Murabaha, Tawarruq, Kafalah, Qard, Qard Hasan, Wakalah, and Ujrah, where applicable. The study also considers relevant AAOIFI Shari'ah Standards and resolutions of recognised Islamic jurisprudence bodies.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
105 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 54 0 R
/E (The research adopts a qualitative, doctrinal, descriptive, analytical, and comparative approach. A supplementary survey component is included through a LinkedIn poll to explore public awareness and opinions regarding Islamic credit cards. Primary sources include the Qur'an, Sunnah, AAOIFI Shari'ah Standards, and relevant resolutions of the International Islamic Fiqh Academy. Secondary sources include academic books, peer-reviewed journal articles, Islamic bank publications, product documents, annual reports, and regulatory publications.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
106 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 54 0 R
/E (The study finds that Islamic credit cards and Islamic charge cards can provide Shariah-oriented alternatives to conventional payment cards when their contractual structures, operations, fees, and payment mechanisms are properly designed and implemented. However, issues relating to certain financing structures, late-payment charges, rewards, cash withdrawals, and differences in scholarly opinions continue to require careful examination. The research also highlights the importance of transparency, effective Shariah governance, customer education, and continuous review of Islamic card products.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
107 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 54 0 R
/E (The study concludes that the question of whether an Islamic card is genuinely Shariah-compliant cannot be answered merely by looking at its name. The underlying contract, actual operation, source of income, treatment of debt, and compliance with relevant Shariah principles must all be examined.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
